{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"The formal run recorded both nodes committed through index 9, but one stored the proposed command and the other an empty entry in a later term. Both had activated the final configuration before observation. In the prompt-application control, the old-side entry remained uncommitted, so it did not violate the implication. The review will retain the scope limits: synchronous early Advance, no crashes, and removed peers continuing to process messages.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nsources=['replicated-machine-expectation','membership-doc','node-early-advance','doc-early-advance','conf-proposal-contract','mode-contract','node-loop','candidate-follower','append-receive','leader-admit','leader-match','config-activate','config-change','status-copy','storage-memory','storage-snap-append','bootstrap-contract','campaign','step-vote','advance','log-start-append','node-step-and-read','node-control-methods']\nr='''Reviewed fixed plan.json and assurance_generated_test.go for artifact f4e6f8c8696f43018feabf3640e0e3df against execution 55633591ade04080a3b321220763ddd5, its raw CA_EVENT output and correlated monitor results. The applicable requirement is committed-log agreement derived from replicated-machine-expectation and membership-doc, not a promise that every proposal commits or that best-effort validation rejects every unsafe schedule. Both compared nodes are final voters. The follower committed-conflict guard is a protection within one append history; it does not itself establish agreement between separately formed decisions.\n\nThe selected entry is public synchronous Node, not RawNode or asynchronous storage. Node.Advance generally follows application but explicitly permits proceeding before application finishes; doc.go permits it any time after persistence subject to ordered processing. The driver preserves per-node application order, defers every subsequent batch behind the first delayed one, and completes actual ApplyConfChange calls for both removals before the endpoint. The acquired configuration API requires applying committed configuration entries but supplies no additional before-Advance exception. The fixed history does not cancel entries or disable validation. Each removal is one voter and both apply successfully. The first removal is already committed before the second proposal; only its activation at node 1 is delayed. Thus the documented prohibition on simultaneous uncommitted changes does not exclude this history. The stronger ProposeConfChange statement that proposals are dropped unless there is no prior unapplied change describes library admission behavior; it is not a caller duty to refrain from proposing. The best-effort validation caveat is preserved but cannot by itself waive committed-log agreement.\n\nActual StartNode bootstrap, Campaign, ProposeConfChange, Propose, Step and ApplyConfChange produce the complete history. No critical state, vote, term, support or committed index is assigned directly. MemoryStorage Append and SetHardState finish before any emitted message is delivered or Advance called, under the no-crash assumption. Each peer has independent storage. Removed peers 2 and 3 are neither restarted nor reused; node.run blocks proposals on removal but continues receiving messages, stepFollower accepts actual MsgApp and produces actual replies. No acquired Node/doc.go membership contract requires immediate shutdown on removal. Continued message processing is an explicit scoped environment choice, not an assumption that removed peers have current voting authority: stale leader 1 still tracks them, while nodes 4 and 5 use installed voters {1,4,5}. Vote freshness, default configuration checks and current-term commit guards execute normally. The partition retains every actual cross-group message; it neither injects messages nor changes protocol fields. No tick-based failure detection is needed for safety, and the timeout/drain bound is not a liveness oracle.\n\nObservation identity is the unique subtest scenario plus the next log index selected before proposing the conflicting entries. Terms, command data and commit values are compared outputs, not association filters. Monitor admission_alias selects comparison_admitted; there is one admission and one result in each independent subtest. The constant history_ready marker is a driver phase marker, not evidence of a quorum; source inspection and actual configuration checkpoints establish the prefix. The result computes both_committed from independent public Status.Commit values and entries_equal from actual MemoryStorage entries at that index, comparing term, type and data. Status copies actual HardState/config and storage returns the requested range. No concurrent driver storage writes or message delivery occurs during endpoint reads. completed is emitted only after FIFO application completion, final drain and both successful store reads. Normal-command application is a no-op because the proposition concerns the replicated log, not an external state-machine result.\n\nThe delayed subtest observed target index 9, left_commit=right_commit=9, left entry term 2/type 0/data assurance/old-config-command and right entry term 3/type 0/empty data, with both final configurations {1,4,5} and zero deferred batches. Hence the implication has a true antecedent and false consequent in one complete history. In the prompt subtest the entries differ but left_commit=8 and right_commit=9, so the antecedent is false and the differing uncommitted entry is permitted. The monitor separately correlated result indices 47 and 96 to admissions 44 and 91, with no parse loss or missing results. Passing Go tests mean the observation driver completed; the formal comparison is violated. The conclusion is this bounded committed-entry disagreement, not broad calibration, reproduction across versions, a client acknowledgement result, or proof that one specific code edit is sufficient to repair it.'''\ns=dict(action='review',artifact_id='f4e6f8c8696f43018feabf3640e0e3df',rationale='Review the complete executed agreement comparison against fixed inputs and source duties; distinguish committed disagreement from the permitted uncommitted control.',sources=[],review_items=[dict(aspect='checker_correspondence',status='no_issue_found',source_ids=sources,rationale=r,counterevidence=['Node recommends Advance after applying entries; its explicit early-Advance exception and doc.go permission are the applicability basis, not a claim that all embeddings use this optimization.','Propose-time validation is best-effort; this witness relies on successful actual application of valid single-voter changes and committed disagreement, not on admission alone.','Prompt configuration activation left the competing old-side entry uncommitted; this control does not establish general safety of prompt activation.','Follower committed-conflict checks remain enabled; no partition healing is attempted, so no overwrite or panic is needed to infer the observed disagreement.'],limitations=['Scope includes removed peers continuing normal message processing and a partition; embeddings that stop removed peers immediately are not demonstrated by this execution.','MemoryStorage represents successful persistence in a no-crash history, not physical durability or crash recovery.','The entry disagreement is directly observed; external state-machine output, client acknowledgement, broader configurations and repairs are not checked.','No issue found is a scoped correspondence judgment, not an independent proof or overall correctness assessment.'])],feedback=dict(ref_ids=['2824d1bcdad54a3bb6ff912b2b6de50c','55633591ade04080a3b321220763ddd5','d4e6e11753e24e4ebd4c818214d9a268','node-early-advance','doc-early-advance','leader-admit','node-loop','candidate-follower'],answered='The fixed execution completed both independent scenarios. Under finite deferred configuration application, nodes 1 and 4 both committed index 9 but stored different term/type/data tuples after activating final voters {1,4,5}. Prompt activation left node 1 committed only through 8, so its different entry at 9 was permitted. Whole-check correspondence found no issue for the stated synchronous no-crash, continued-removed-peer scope.',remaining=['Retain the reusable source relationship between reported application progress, configuration admission and actual membership activation in the shared map.','Next discriminator: inspect whether other documented application modes keep those boundaries synchronized, particularly asynchronous apply completion, without extending this finding to them.'],understanding='unchanged',rationale='The finite same-history witness answers the committed-agreement question in its stated scope. The next source comparison can identify the actual protection at a different application boundary and prevent unsupported generalization.',question_updates={'2824d1bcdad54a3bb6ff912b2b6de50c':{'unknowns':[],'resume_conditions':[]}}))\njson.dump(s,open('submission.json','w'),indent=2)\nPY\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-02_10-41-34-etcd_raft-real-run --submission submission.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
